= Introduction <sec:intro>

Locks remain the most common way systems software serializes access to shared state. The fairness they usually promise is FIFO acquisition: waiters receive the lock in the order they asked for it. Patel et al. showed that this promise is not enough @patel2020scl. When threads hold a contended lock for different lengths of time and spend much of their time holding it, the lock, not the CPU scheduler, decides how processor time is divided: each thread's share follows its hold time, whatever the scheduler intends. They called this _scheduler subversion_ and proposed _lock usage fairness_, which divides lock hold time rather than acquisitions in proportion to each thread's scheduling weight. In UpScaleDB @upscaledb, an embedded key-value store, they found that insert threads received nearly six times the CPU time of find threads.

Usage-fair locks solve this problem. Patel et al.’s u-SCL enforces usage fairness with two mechanisms @patel2020scl. It grants ownership in _lock slices_ of a few milliseconds, and it tracks each thread's lock usage and _bans_ a thread whose usage exceeds its share, keeping it from acquiring the lock until the other active threads have received sufficient lock time. A slice is dedicated to its owner: the owner may release and reacquire the lock freely, and no other thread may acquire it until the slice expires, even while the owner is outside its critical section. u-SCL is therefore not _work-conserving_: the lock can sit idle while other threads wait. CFL (Completely Fair Locking) removes this limitation @park2024cfl. It applies the policy of Linux's Completely Fair Scheduler to locks: just as CFS runs the task with the smallest virtual runtime, CFL tracks a priority-weighted _virtual lock hold time_ per thread and reorders the wait queue so that the waiter with the smallest value acquires next. Because it only reorders waiters and never bans them, the lock is never idle while a thread waits.

Both locks, however, share a property with almost every lock: the thread that is served runs its own critical section. This assumption creates a dilemma between fairness and locality. A lock's performance depends heavily on locality: a critical section runs fastest when the protected data is already in the running core's cache, which is the case when the lock's previous holder ran on the same core. A hand-off to a thread on another core instead forces the data to migrate, one cache-to-cache transfer for every line the critical section touches. Chiplet processors, which split a socket into dies with private last-level caches @naffziger2021chiplet, make transfers between dies costlier still. #todo[cite or measure cross-die vs. same-die transfer latency, e.g. AMD EPYC CCD-to-CCD vs. intra-CCX] Fairness, however, forces hand-offs. Locks built for speed therefore choose locality. `parking_lot` @parkinglot, a widely used Rust library of compact, fast synchronization primitives, provides a `Mutex` that lets a releasing thread take the lock straight back and forces a hand-off to the longest waiter only about every 0.5 ms, while its strictly FIFO `FairMutex` is documented as "generally slower, but sometimes needed".

Neither u-SCL nor CFL resolves this dilemma; each gives up one side of it. u-SCL keeps locality by reserving the lock for its slice owner, and so leaves the lock idle while others wait. CFL keeps the lock busy and fair, and so hands it from core to core, moving the data with it.

_Delegation_ locks, a special kind of lock, escape this dilemma by breaking the assumption behind it. In flat combining @hendler2010fc, each thread publishes its critical section as a request in a per-lock publication list, and the thread that acquires the lock becomes the _combiner_ and executes all pending requests on its own core; the protected data stays in one cache regardless of whose request runs. Successors of flat combining, up to TCLocks @gupta2023tclocks, which delegates unmodified lock-based critical sections transparently, use this separation for speed and transparency but serve requests in the order they were published, and so remain as usage-unfair as first-come-first-served locks. Once execution no longer follows the lock, however, choosing _whose_ request runs next becomes a pure scheduling decision, and the combiner, which executes every request, can measure each one's cost for the price of two timestamp reads.

We build on this observation with FC-PQ (flat combining with a priority queue), a usage-fair delegation lock. Its combiner keeps pending requests in a priority queue and serves the one whose thread has accumulated the least critical-section time, in the spirit of virtual-time fair queueing @demers1989wfq, and an uncontended fast path lets a thread that finds no pending requests execute directly, bypassing the publication protocol. FC-PQ pursues SCL's goal in work-conserving form: whenever a request is pending, the combiner runs one. To reason about when this pays, we develop an analysis framework that splits the price of fairness into accounting (measuring each thread's service), migration, idleness (the lock left unused while threads wait), and redistribution (service moved from some threads to others), and shows that delegation leaves mainly the first and last.

Work conservation, however, exposes a subtler cost of fairness, which has nothing to do with where the data lives. A lock, or a combiner, can choose only among the requests that are waiting when it decides, yet a thread may want the lock without currently waiting for it: it may still be waking from a futex wait, or it may have been preempted. The lock must then pay for this _absent_ thread in one of three ways. It can hold the lock for the thread, as u-SCL's dedicated slice does, and leave the lock idle. It can give the turn to whoever is waiting, as CFL does, and let the absent thread fall behind; CFL even lets a newly arriving thread take the lock ahead of a sleeping waiter to hide the waiter's wake-up delay, which its authors note can significantly degrade fairness @park2024cfl. Or it can have waiters spin instead of sleep, so that none is ever absent, and pay in CPU time. No lock design avoids this cost; a lock can only choose how to pay it.

We evaluate FC-PQ inside redb @redb, an embedded transactional key-value store that admits one write transaction at a time, and inside UpScaleDB. Service time is charged to the requesting thread even when the combiner executes the request, and core frequency is pinned so that DVFS cannot favor one lock's waiting policy over another's. In redb, the FIFO MCS queue lock @mellorcrummey1991mcs gives 1-record and 64-record writers equal numbers of turns, yet the 64-record writers hold the lock about 70% of the time, for a Jain fairness index @jain1984fairness over per-thread critical-section time of 0.85--0.86. FC-PQ raises it to 0.94--0.995, near u-SCL's 1.000, at the throughput of redb's built-in `std::sync` `Mutex`/`Condvar` writer gate, with 10--18 cache-to-cache transfers per transaction where MCS incurs 93--126. Our prototype's waiters spin, which costs CPU time and limits it to no more threads than cores; this is an implementation choice, not a property of usage-fair delegation, since waiting and serving are independent and delegation locks can block their waiters until the request completes @gupta2023tclocks. On redb's back-to-back writers FC-PQ only ties u-SCL, whose slices waste nothing when threads never leave the lock. We expect FC-PQ to pull ahead when threads do real work between critical sections and when the protected data is large; Section 8 states these predictions.

This paper makes the following contributions:

- We show that the cost of lock usage fairness comes from coupling who is served with where the critical section runs, which forces a fair lock to pay in migration or, if it reserves the lock, in idleness.
- We give an analysis framework that decomposes the price of fairness and predicts when delegation lowers it.
- We design FC-PQ, a work-conserving, usage-fair delegation lock with a fast path proven sound for the uncontended case.
- We evaluate FC-PQ in redb and UpScaleDB with service charged to the requester and a fixed CPU clock, and report which costs of fairness delegation removes and which remain.

Section 2 reviews lock usage fairness; Section 3 develops the analysis framework; Section 4 presents FC-PQ; Section 5 describes our methodology; Sections 6 and 7 evaluate redb and UpScaleDB; Section 8 discusses limitations and predictions; Section 9 covers related work.
